# Adjoint Selmer Rigidity: An Interactive Journey

Begin with the central geometric question: can an arithmetic object move while satisfying every linearized global and local condition? The relation map sends a candidate velocity to its obstruction, and its kernel is the tangent space.

## 1. Detecting motion by exact rank

Read the exact rank demonstration, then run it to compare a rigid rectangular map with a flexible one.

{{demo:0}}

The general computational method is exact row reduction. It does more than return a Boolean answer: in the non-rigid case it constructs the missing tangent directions.

{{algorithm:0}}

## 2. Rigidity as a determinantal condition

For square presentations, injectivity, surjectivity, and nonzero determinant coincide. Explore the transition continuously.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The accompanying plot turns the same equation into a geometric picture.

{{visualization:0}}

Edit the entries yourself and observe that a single determinant governs every equivalent condition.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

For rectangular maps, maximal minors replace the determinant.

{{algorithm:1}}

The two-parameter heat map shows how rank-drop equations carve out a determinantal subvariety rather than a random exceptional set.

{{visualization:1}}

## 3. Enlarging coefficients

A nonzero maximal minor remains nonzero in every extension field. This certificate is the finite-dimensional face of flat scalar extension.

{{algorithm:2}}

The exact numerical demonstrations in the full program also compare rigidity before and after rational scalar extension.

{{demo:1}}

## 4. Contragredient symmetry

The arithmetic mirror reverses weight coordinates and negates them. Apply the mirror twice and the original variation returns.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

The concise numerical demonstration confirms involutivity and shows that the dual is zero exactly when the original variation is zero.

{{demo:2}}

## 5. Synthesis

A vanished adjoint Selmer space, once identified with the tangent kernel, means that every admissible velocity is zero. Exact rank reveals this rigidity, determinants organize its jumping locus, scalar extension preserves it, and contragredient duality reflects it. Together the widgets move from matrix evidence to the geometric structure of automorphic deformation problems.

